closed	p2	Backend stop/restart is very slow (~90s) on every deploy: uvicorn graceful-shutdown blocks on open SSE streams. Symptom: ansible 'fastapi_backend : Restart backend service' handler and plain 'systemctl stop new-archon-backend' hang for ~1.5min. ROOT CAUSE: on SIGTERM uvicorn stops accepting then waits (default timeout_graceful_shutdown=None = indefinitely) for active connections to finish, but the /stream SSE generators (main.py:509) are long-lived and never end on their own. The app DOES try to close them gracefully - lifespan-shutdown sets _shutdown_event + _wake_sse_connections() (main.py:372) and the SSE loop polls the event every <=1s (main.py:708-730) - BUT ASGI lifespan-shutdown runs only AFTER uvicorn has drained connections, i.e. after it is already blocked on those same streams, so the signal is too late. systemd then SIGKILLs at its default TimeoutStopSec=90s. service.j2:12 sets no --timeout-graceful-shutdown and the unit has no TimeoutStopSec/KillMode. FIXES to evaluate (prefer the first): (1) add uvicorn '--timeout-graceful-shutdown 5' to ExecStart so it force-closes lingering streams after a short grace instead of waiting forever; (2) install a SIGTERM handler that sets _shutdown_event immediately (before uvicorn starts draining) so streams self-close within ~1s; (3) systemd TimeoutStopSec=10 + KillMode=mixed as a backstop. Likely (1)+(2) together. Not a correctness bug (server does come back via Restart=always) - just makes every beta/prod deploy and manual restart slow. Affects #39 prod deploys too. Surfaced during #91 beta deploys. relates:#91 relates:#39
closed	p2	SSE catch-up sends each object type as ONE unbounded `data:` line (main.py ~589 ",".join(json_strings)); the browser EventSource has no per-line cap so the frontend is fine, but the bot's aiohttp StreamReader caps a line at 512KB (524288) and raises 'Got more than 524288 bytes when reading' ~90ms into every /stream connect once a type's catch-up exceeds 512KB (hit on beta with 8096 tournaments). Compounded by a defeated bot backoff: sse_listener._sse_loop resets retry_delay=1 right after the 200, BEFORE the read fails, so every iteration reconnects+fails+sleeps only 1s -> steady ~1/sec storm that churns the backend pool (overlay conn returned ACTIVE -> 'another command is already in progress' -> discard BAD). FIX A (server, real fix): bound every SSE catch-up/overlay frame by bytes (chunk json lists to ~200KB/line) - also converts the overlay count-based 500/100 batches + the unbounded organizer-decks/own-decks lines. Matters for #39 (far more data). FIX B (bot): only reset backoff after a healthy sync (sync_complete), and sleep on every reconnect path, so a fast-failing read cannot hammer. Surfaced in #91 §6.5 first bot-SSE exercise; blocks #39 (prod has way more tournaments). relates:#91 relates:#39 parent:#35
closed	p2	SSE personal-overlay phase holds a pooled DB connection across its yields (main.py stream_updates, the 'if not scoped and stream_user ...' block ~:717-810): it opens 'async with _pool.connection() as db_conn' then yields SSE frames to the client INSIDE that block. While suspended at a yield the connection is checked out; if the client disconnects mid-overlay the generator is cancelled while a query is in flight, returning an ACTIVE connection to the pool -> 'another command is already in progress' on the pool's reset-rollback -> connection discarded as BAD. This is the deeper root cause of the pool-churn seen in #179 (there it was amplified by the bot's 1-2/s reconnect storm; the storm is fixed, but a browser disconnecting mid-overlay still churns one connection). FIX: adopt the shape of _scoped_catchup_frames (added in #179) - buffer all overlay rows into a list under the connection, release the connection, THEN yield. The catch-up phase (stream_objects_new) already releases before yielding; only the overlay holds across yields. Pre-existing (not introduced by #179); flagged by principal-engineer review of #179. relates:#179 relates:#178
