closed	p2	Snapshot meta.timestamp could outrun rows the snapshot never captured -> silent permanent staleness on the bootstrap path. generate_snapshots seeded the client's since cursor with max(modified_at) over the rows actually read, but stream_objects_snapshot opens ONE explicit transaction per (type, level) (db.py), so each type is consistent as of its OWN MVCC instant and the max is a max over DIFFERENT instants. Concrete miss with OBJECT_TYPES order (user, sanction, tournament, deck, league, promo): user cursor opens at t=0; a profile edit at t=3s is invisible to it; the tournament cursor opens at t=8s and reads a row written at t=6s; meta.timestamp=6s, so the client connects with since=6s and the t=3s user row is NEVER delivered. generated_at does not rescue it (main.py uses it only for the >3-day staleness guard, never to filter modified_at), so the row stays stale until it is modified again or an access-version change / >3-day absence forces a resync - no self-heal, no client-side detection. Same mechanism for tombstones: a row soft-deleted AFTER its type's transaction opens ships as live, and if the tombstone loses the since race the client keeps a ghost object; after 30 days purge_deleted_objects hard-deletes the row so no delta can ever repair it. Cross-type gap was pre-existing, but #514 widened it twice: the old keyset path self-healed within a type (a row updated mid-scan got a modified_at ahead of the cursor and was re-read in a later batch with fresh content - a per-type cursor snapshot cannot), and the tombstone window grew from 'deleted after being read' to 'deleted after the type's transaction opened'. FIXED: anchor the cursor to generated_at (SELECT now() taken BEFORE any read, so every per-type MVCC snapshot is >= it) - any row modified after it is either in the file (harmless re-delivery) or strictly after the cursor. Rejected the #517 sketch of max(modified_at) taken up front in one REPEATABLE READ txn: correct, but there is no usable index f